{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1369572-hit-mark-vs-a-werewolf-firearms-question","id":1369572,"title":"HIT Mark vs. a werewolf (firearms question?)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-22T07:11:03-08:00","id":1369572,"title":"HIT Mark vs. a werewolf (firearms question?)","content":"One thing I remember about the Storytellers guide to the technocracy is that there was a little blurb about playing a HIT Mark. It was basically not advised because they were perceived as too tough, and there was a joke about having a place to hang a bunch of Get of Fenris pelts.<br /> <br /> Were they really that tough? I can only imagine that they had a pretty good soak (9 dice), and a Chain-gun which, I assume, will be able to tear through werewolves.<br /> <br /> How would that work, would we assume that the bullets are silver or just made of Primium (which, in an earlier book, I am pretty sure was aggravated, but you could soak). The stats for a Chain-gun are Difficulty 7, Rate 3, Can fire full auto bursts and 3 round bursts, Damage 8. Looking at the dice pool for a HIT Mark in 20th edition it looks like their Dex+Firearms would be about 5. If a HIT Mark was shooting at a werewolf, what would be most efficient? One shots, 3 round bursts, or Full Auto?<br /> <br /> Futhermore, how does rate and multiple actions work for Firearms? I tried finding the rules but the M20 Book is 600 pages and I am getting lost trying to find it. I imagine that maybe firing 3, 3 round bursts would deal the most damage but what is the actual rule for firing multiple rounds at the same target?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-23T13:57:51-08:00","id":1369903,"title":"","content":"So, I could be incorrect, but from what I understand Rate is the Maximum number of times you can fire a weapon a turn if you have extra actions.<br /> <br /> But yeah. Full Auto and Burst Fire just adds flat damage boosts in exchange for being harder to land.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-23T18:33:01-08:00","id":1369941,"title":"","content":"Under a surprisingly large number of situations, the chaingun and other built-in weapons will be irrelevant in a fight against Garou or Fera, because there is an array of Gifts for disabling and bricking technology, and the math from Primium-coating countermagick does not work out so well for the HIT Mark under most situations. Four dice at difficulty 8 versus five or more at difficulty 4 or 5 - even with doubling the Primium roll's successes, that's not really a wise wager.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-24T03:58:13-08:00","id":1370024,"title":"","content":"Eh. Built in weapons are artifacts. Vulgar ones at that. I wouldn't allow them to be effected by Jam technology, because strictly speaking, they aren't. They're magic.<br /> <br /> Plus, to stop guns from firing you would still be looking at difficulty 8.<br /> <br /> Plus the fifty foot range limitation. There's a good chance they're dead before reaching 50 feet of the Hitmark. Not 100% but at least 50/50.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-24T11:34:22-08:00","id":1370106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1369572-hit-mark-vs-a-werewolf-firearms-question?p=1370024#post1370024\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh. Built in weapons are artifacts. Vulgar ones at that. I wouldn't allow them to be effected by Jam technology, because strictly speaking, they aren't. They're magic.<br /> <br /> Plus, to stop guns from firing you would still be looking at difficulty 8.<br /> <br /> Plus the fifty foot range limitation. There's a good chance they're dead before reaching 50 feet of the Hitmark. Not 100% but at least 50/50.</div> </div> </div> </div>Firstly, shutting the gun down is an unnecessarily precise approach for Jam Technology. You'd just have to shut down the computer processors that allow the cyborg to function as, well, a cyborg; as these are presumably some really advanced integrated circuits, that's difficulty 4, and has the benefit of bringing said cyborg to a grinding halt, possibly even ruining it for good as life support systems for the organic parts fail to function.<br /> <br /> Secondly, the range limitation isn't much of a limitation, because most places where a combat cyborg would freely operate aren't going to be wide-open, and those that are generally have different issues for said cyborg, such as a sudden sinkhole opening up, surprise quicksand, flash flooding, and other terrain complications.<br /> <br /> Thirdly, the specialized Gifts that specifically target the chaingun are typically used at a lower difficulty, and might have a longer range. Jam Weapon a first rank CoG favorite, works out to vocal range and has a difficulty based on the Willpower of the weapon's user. Jam Gun from Werewolf: the Wyld West is also less burdened. Gremlins is more burdened, but as a benefit, anything it works against is ruined for good.<br /> <br /> As for the issue of &quot;artifacts&quot;, you can't have it both ways, by declaring that technology is magic in setting, but then claim that it's magic but not subject to being grouped as technology by spirits. Too bad ItX skimps on that area, else the quick circumventing of their tech might not work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T16:56:16-08:00","id":1370470,"title":"","content":"Most of the mechanics of Mage don't match the fluff. For example, Hard Suits are supposed to be able to solo a nest of vampires with ease. Unfortunately, the armor mechanics and monster stats in WoD are awful and being outnumber punishes you twice for some reason, so that'd never happen. So in fluff, a Hit Mark would beat the ever living crap out of a werewolf. In mechanics, it's a toss up because who is surprising whom is the biggest thing that matters in WoD combat.<br /> Personally, I tend to buff the ever living crap Countermagick and armor so it's not useless and make Hit Marks akin to actual magical terminators to match the lore. Bullets and fireballs should be needed in droves to actually hurt them cause they're robots with Matter enforced bodies wielding super science guns. It should take an entire cabal working together to drop one unless they're really prepared or have a massive advantage, like being in a chantry.<br /> If the technocrats send a Hit Mark after you, you deserve everything coming to you.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T18:29:06-08:00","id":1370478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>theoutlander523</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1369572-hit-mark-vs-a-werewolf-firearms-question?p=1370470#post1370470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most of the mechanics of Mage don't match the fluff. For example, Hard Suits are supposed to be able to solo a nest of vampires with ease. Unfortunately, the armor mechanics and monster stats in WoD are awful and being outnumber punishes you twice for some reason, so that'd never happen. So in fluff, a Hit Mark would beat the ever living crap out of a werewolf.</div> </div> </div> </div>Only in Technocracy propaganda pieces. Besides, vampires are kind of fragile, so that's not really a useful metric on the part of the hypertech device in question. Try fighting something that has over five dice to soak aggravated damage and doesn't burn up if you compromise their shelter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In mechanics, it's a toss up because who is surprising whom is the biggest thing that matters in WoD combat.<br /> Personally, I tend to buff the ever living crap Countermagick and armor so it's not useless and make Hit Marks akin to actual magical terminators to match the lore. Bullets and fireballs should be needed in droves to actually hurt them cause they're robots with Matter enforced bodies wielding super science guns. It should take an entire cabal working together to drop one unless they're really prepared or have a massive advantage, like being in a chantry.<br /> If the technocrats send a Hit Mark after you, you deserve everything coming to you. </div> </div> </div>Be careful what you wish for with the term &quot;actual terminators&quot;, since the first movie ended up with a T-800 getting crushed in a pedestrian hydraulic press. It wouldn't be out of the question for Garou or Fera of mightier bent to simply just duplicate that feat on a HIT Mark with their hands, or under foot, for large Mokole. Might of Thor, Gurahl Rage, Fu Xi's Honor... don't underestimate the capacity of werebeasts to jack their stats up even higher than Crinos would suggest and into the sky. If a Fenrir has Fenris as a totem and Might of Thor, that's Strength 20, and subsequently, a basement full of parts that would do Sarah Connor proud.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-27T09:50:57-08:00","id":1370782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>theoutlander523</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1369572-hit-mark-vs-a-werewolf-firearms-question?p=1370470#post1370470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most of the mechanics of Mage don't match the fluff. For example, Hard Suits are supposed to be able to solo a nest of vampires with ease. Unfortunately, the armor mechanics and monster stats in WoD are awful and being outnumber punishes you twice for some reason, so that'd never happen. So in fluff, a Hit Mark would beat the ever living crap out of a werewolf. In mechanics, it's a toss up because who is surprising whom is the biggest thing that matters in WoD combat.<br /> Personally, I tend to buff the ever living crap Countermagick and armor so it's not useless and make Hit Marks akin to actual magical terminators to match the lore. Bullets and fireballs should be needed in droves to actually hurt them cause they're robots with Matter enforced bodies wielding super science guns. It should take an entire cabal working together to drop one unless they're really prepared or have a massive advantage, like being in a chantry.<br /> If the technocrats send a Hit Mark after you, you deserve everything coming to you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Part of the issue there is comparing the mass production suits to some particularly operatives souped up personal suit.<br /> <br /> Technocratic gear is much closer to the fluff when you make it yourself.<br /> <br /> Same for HITmarks.<br /> <br /> Requisitions is useful. Don't get me wrong. But if you let it front load everything what's the point in actually being enlightened?<br /> <br /> Also, by and large under those rules all you would need to do is carry a Spirit Ban device to render Garou impotent, and considering Iteration-X was having something of a Renaissance of Dim Sci after the DA and a Spirit Ban is relatively simple to accomplish. You're back to square one of 'Who goes first?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}